# The reader's own count of the two JUDGED DOCUMENTS, pinned, and the flag that
# goes and asks for a new one (design.md §1.6; panels 011, 123, 134).
#
# **Its own module since 2026-09-12, and the seam is a question rather than a
# line count.** `cli/measure.hero` answers *what does this file measure on the
# tables we carry* — offline, from the working directory, for any path. This one
# answers *what did the tokeniser that actually reads it say, and when*: a
# different input (the network), a different cadence (by hand, never a check)
# and a different subject. The two were one file until `cli/measure.hero` passed
# §11's threshold, and the split had to be argued rather than rounded to; the
# argument is this paragraph.
#
# **TWO DOCUMENTS SINCE 2026-09-12, and this paragraph used to say "one document
# and no other".** Panel 134 measured `CLAUDE.md` at 7306 real against a stated
# ceiling of 5500 and found it had been **1806 over for three days**, because the
# contract alone was judged on the maximum over two VENDORED tables whose larger
# is OpenAI's `cl100k_base` — while `heroes measure` printed `Headroom: 9`. That
# is §1.6's own history one document across: the spec was 998 over a line it was
# told it was 40 under. The subject of this module is therefore *the documents a
# ceiling judges*, which is two.
#
# **They are two documents and not one list, because their ceilings do different
# jobs** (author, ratifying panel 134). The spec's ceiling bears on the LANGUAGE:
# every token is a feature paying rent, and squeezing is the point. The
# contract's is HOUSEKEEPING — its repair is never to cut a rule but to move one
# into `.claude/rules/`, where it loads only when its paths are touched, so what
# it measures is the fixed cost paid on every turn. One instrument, two jobs.
#
# **Which file is which stays with the tool that defaults to one of them.** This
# module is handed the path rather than holding it, because it decides nothing
# about which document the tool measures and everything about what was
# measured.
#
# THE GATE MAY NOT TOUCH THE NETWORK (panel 123 R1 and R5). design.md Part 11
# metric 1 forbids it in as many words, and the repository being public means
# GitHub passes no secrets to a fork's pull request, so a key-gated check is
# structurally red on every fork PR forever. So the number is recorded, with
# the model that produced it and the day it was taken, and `heroes measure
# --refresh` is what goes and asks. It is the shape Bazel's `sha256`, pip's
# hash-checking mode and Nix's fixed-output derivations all take, and Go's
# `toolchain` line is the precedent for writing the instrument's name beside
# the pinned data, *"for reproducibility"*.
#
# THE DIGEST IS WHAT MAKES A STALE RECORD LOUD. It is the spec's content hash,
# and when it does not match the file in hand the verdict is WITHHELD rather
# than computed — because a headroom sentence against a stale count is the
# exact sentence that cost this project 998 tokens.

use cli/io
use cli/refresh
use cli/count_tokens
use cli/table as cli_table

constant SPEC_REAL_TOKENS: i64
    7531

constant SPEC_REAL_MODEL: str
    "claude-opus-5"

constant SPEC_REAL_TAKEN: str
    "2026-09-12"

constant SPEC_DIGEST: str
    "0b96e29b3ca14666"

# The contract's record. **It carries no digest, and the refusal is measured**
# (panel 134, the compiler seat's veto). A digest withholds the verdict when the
# file in hand is not the file the count was taken of, which costs the spec
# nothing: an edit there already needs a panel and a ledger row. `CLAUDE.md` took
# **34 commits in seven days** against the spec's 17, amended by author
# instruction with no panel, so a digest here would withhold the verdict about
# thirty-four times a week — each clearable only by a network refresh, and **not
# clearable at all on a fork PR**, which is panel 123 R1's own refusal arriving
# through the window.
#
# What replaces it is `CONTRACT_TOKENS`, the vendored count taken in the same
# refresh. It is keyless, offline and fork-clean, it is the detector
# `suite_spec.hero` already certifies as having *"always answered correctly"* at
# the job of *did this document move and nobody say so*, and it is also the
# previous level the delta gate subtracts from. One pin, three jobs.
constant CONTRACT_REAL_TOKENS: i64
    7402

constant CONTRACT_REAL_MODEL: str
    "claude-opus-5"

constant CONTRACT_REAL_TAKEN: str
    "2026-09-12"

constant CONTRACT_TOKENS: i64
    5556

# `--refresh`: ask the reader's own tokeniser and print the record to paste.
#
# THE ARTIFACT IS THE FOUR CONSTANTS, ON STDOUT, and the instruction that goes
# with them is on stderr — `.claude/rules/cli-surface.md`'s contract, and here
# it earns itself: a reader can redirect stdout into a file and get eight lines
# that compile.
#
# AND IT REFUSES EVERY FILE THAT NO CEILING JUDGES. The record belongs to the
# judged documents. A digest of another file is a digest nothing cross-checks,
# printed beside a count nothing judges: a measurement in the shape of one, which
# is the class this whole flag exists to close. The refusal is the rule; what
# changed on 2026-09-12 is that there are two judged documents rather than one,
# and the refusal now names both so a reader learns which they are.
function run_refresh(path: str, text: str, spec: str, contract: str, vendored: i64) -> cli_table.Exit
    if path != spec && path != contract
        io.eprint(
            text: "error: `--refresh` refreshes the record for `" + spec + "` and `" + contract + "`, and `" + path + "` has none — no ceiling judges it and no check keeps it honest (panel 123 R5)"
        )
        return .failed_exit
    lines = refresh.refreshed(text: text, spec: path == spec, vendored: vendored)

    if lines.is_err()
        io.eprint(text: "error: " + refresh.why_lines(lines))
        return .failed_exit

    for row in lines.must()
        print(row)

    io.eprint(
        text: "note: paste these into `tests/harness/suite_spec.hero` AND `selfhost/measure/pinned.hero`, in ONE commit — the two copies are what the harness cross-checks against what this tool prints. The names carry the document, so a paste cannot land in the other one's record"
    )
    return .ok_exit

test "the model in each record is the model the refresh asks"
    assert SPEC_REAL_MODEL == count_tokens.PINNED_MODEL
    assert CONTRACT_REAL_MODEL == count_tokens.PINNED_MODEL

# The digest was a placeholder for one build, and this is what stopped it
# staying one. It is kept rather than deleted: a record whose digest is all
# zeroes is a count of nothing, and the day somebody pastes a number without
# pasting what it was a count OF, this fires.
test "the record's digest is a real one and not the placeholder"
    assert SPEC_DIGEST != "0000000000000000"
    assert SPEC_DIGEST.len() == 16

test "the refresh refuses every file no ceiling judges, on the path and not on the key"
    # The half of `--refresh` a test can reach with no key in the environment:
    # the refusal happens on the PATH, before anything asks the network.
    #
    # **`CLAUDE.md` was on this list until 2026-09-12 and has moved sides**, which
    # is panel 134 in one line. It is not deleted from the case: a file that no
    # ceiling judges is still refused, and `design.md` is the witness for that —
    # §1.6 says in its own words that *"the spec has a budget; the compiler does
    # not"*, so measuring it is a count and never a verdict.
    assert cli_table.exit_code(run_refresh(path: "design.md", text: "x", spec: "spec/heroes-spec.md", contract: "CLAUDE.md", vendored: 0)) == 2
    assert cli_table.exit_code(run_refresh(path: "docs/ROADMAP.md", text: "x", spec: "spec/heroes-spec.md", contract: "CLAUDE.md", vendored: 0)) == 2
